    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.starter\n\n### Description\n\nI am replacing [alpha](https://github.com/goolord/alpha-nvim) with mini.starter and, so far, I really like the results.\r\n\r\nOne thing that got me a little confused at the start was the whole 'querying' for the items. I understand now how it works, but I don't think that's the optimal approach for a start screen (in my case, at least). \r\n\r\nFor the sake of this example, let's say I have the following items on my start screen:\r\n\r\n```\r\n- [N]ew File\r\n- [Gi]t status\r\n- [F]ind File\r\n- [Gr]ep Text\r\n- [Q]uit\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe characters enclosed in `[]` are the characters highlighted by `mini.starter` that will trigger that command. As you can see, Git and Grep both require two keystrokes instead of one, as they share the first letter.\r\n\r\nI would like to have an option to set each item 'hotkey' and pressing that hotkey would trigger the item, for example:\r\n\r\n```\r\n - [N]ew file\r\n - [G]it status\r\n - [F]ind file\r\n - G[r]ep Text\r\n - [Q]uit\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis would make <kbd>g</kbd> trigger the `Git` item and <kbd>r</kbd> trigger the `Grep Text` item.\r\n\r\nSince the items in the dashboard aren't changing all that much (pretty much never), querying for an item becomes a bit cumbersome, as if I had the option to set the shortcut for that item directly would be much more efficient.\r\n\r\nI understand that this might affect the querying system as it might cause conflicting maps, but for my personal case, I don't mind losing query capabilities in favor of this, so an opt-in configuration would be fine.",
